## User preferences

- when the user asked whether Twyne could publish “posts from twyne to other sites” and then said “get standard site ready” -> give a direct capability boundary, then implement and validate the requested Standard.site path rather than stopping at conceptual advice [Task 1][Task 2]

## Reusable knowledge

- Twyne already shares at `/{handle}/{slug}` and writes ATProto records, but it has no generic WordPress/Ghost/Medium/Substack/Micropub/webhook destination or per-writer custom-domain system. Standard.site is an interoperability schema, not automatic publishing to arbitrary sites. `sesame`, not “sessame”, is the installed Porkbun DNS CLI. [Task 1]
- Canonical helpers are `src/utils/standard-site-paths.ts`; `src/utils/standard-site.ts` creates/reuses publications, creates/updates documents while preserving record keys, loads records, and unpublishes. PDS-backed readers are in `src/utils/standard-site-reader.server.ts`; pages live under `/at/<encoded DID>/<publication rkey>` and its document-rkey child. [Task 2]
- Expose `rel="site.standard.publication"` on publication pages, `rel="site.standard.document"` on document pages, and the publication AT-URI from the catch-all `/.well-known/site.standard.publication/...` route. Existing `twyne.love` and wildcard DNS already point to Railway, but writer custom-domain onboarding remains unimplemented. [Task 2]
- Local evidence: 9 focused tests, typecheck, lint, Prettier, diff check, client/server builds, and missing-record HTTP 404 checks passed. [Task 2]

## Failures and how to do differently

- Symptom: reader renders missing PDS content with HTTP 200. Fix: explicitly call Qwik `status(404)` in loaders when publication/document resolution fails. [Task 2]
- The full suite had one pre-existing order-dependent Fish Audio failure; it passed in isolation. Vet/diff review stalled without findings. Changes were uncommitted, undeployed, and never used an authorized PDS session, so do not call Standard.site production-ready or live-published. [Task 2]

